
Crucible harness API (author a FIXTURE only — no test fns):

- Imports:
    use crucible_fuzzer::*;                          // TestContext, macros, fuzz_assert_*
    use crucible_fuzzer::anchor_lang::system_program;
    use {{ crate_id }}::*;                               // instruction, accounts, ID, state types
    use solana_keypair::Keypair; use solana_pubkey::Pubkey; use solana_signer::Signer;
    use std::rc::Rc;

- The fixture struct MUST be named `Fixture` and derive Clone; keypairs go in `Rc`:
    #[derive(Clone)]
    struct Fixture {
        ctx: TestContext, program_id: Pubkey, /* pdas, users (Rc<Keypair>) */
        pub(crate) accepted: Accepted,   // what the negative actions recorded — see below
    }
    #[derive(Clone, Default)]
    pub(crate) struct Accepted { /* one bool per negative action, named after the attempt */ }

- #[fuzz_fixture] impl Fixture { ... } with:
    pub fn setup() -> Self {
        let mut ctx = TestContext::new();
        let program_id = Pubkey::new_from_array(ID.to_bytes());
        ctx.add_program(&program_id, PROGRAM_SO).unwrap();  // `PROGRAM_SO` is declared for you by
        // the crate root and is the ONLY way to name the program's `.so` — do not write a path.
        // create funded accounts: ctx.create_account().pubkey(kp.pubkey())
        //     .lamports(N).owner(system_program::ID).create().unwrap();
        // derive PDAs: Pubkey::find_program_address(&[b"seed", key.as_ref()], &program_id)
        // run any init instruction (see calling convention). Panic on setup failure.
        Self { ctx, program_id, /* ... */ }
    }
    // one `action_<name>` per instruction; fuzzable args get #[range(lo..hi)]:
    pub fn action_<name>(&mut self, #[range(0..1_000_000)] amount: u64) -> bool {
        self.ctx.program(self.program_id)
            .call(instruction::<Name> { amount })
            .accounts(accounts::<Name> { /* fields */ })
            .signers(&[&*self.some_keypair])
            .send().map(|o| o.is_success()).unwrap_or(false)
    }
    // The `-> bool` is "did this ACTION do what it was designed to do". For the positive action
    // above that is the instruction succeeding. For a NEGATIVE action — one that attempts something
    // the program must reject — making the attempt is the action working, so it returns `true`,
    // and it RECORDS the outcome instead of asserting on it:
    pub fn action_<name>_unauthorized(&mut self) -> bool {
        let res = self.ctx.program(self.program_id)
            .call(instruction::<Name> { /* ... */ })
            .accounts(accounts::<Name> { /* the wrong signer */ })
            .signers(&[&*self.other_signer])
            .send();
        self.accepted.<name>_unauthorized |= res.map(|o| o.is_success()).unwrap_or(false);
        true   // NOT false — see below
    }
    // Return `false` only when the action could not be attempted at all. Crucible treats `false` as
    // a dead-end state and STOPS the action sequence there, so a negative action returning `false`
    // truncates every campaign that draws it.

- Anything fallible that a FUZZED value reaches must be handled, never unwrapped. The one that
  bites hardest is PDA derivation: use `Pubkey::try_find_program_address(...)` and `return false`
  on `None` — `find_program_address` PANICS when no bump yields a valid address.
  A panic here is not a finding and is not reported as one. It happens outside the fuzz target, so
  the fuzzer records no crash, the process aborts, and EVERY property in that campaign comes back
  as an error rather than a verdict. Worse, the input that reached it is written to the shared
  corpus, so every LATER component's campaign loads it and dies the same way — one unchecked
  derivation takes down the whole run, not one test.
  Seeds fixed when you write them (`b"lma"`, a key created in `setup()`) cannot fail this way; the
  rule is for seeds an `action_*` argument or fuzzed byte reaches. In `setup()` itself, panicking
  is still fine — that is the harness failing before any campaign starts.

- The fixture NEVER asserts a property: no `fuzz_assert*` inside an `action_*`, and no
  `"[<property title>] ..."` message anywhere in it. The fixture is built into EVERY component's
  fuzz target, so an assertion here fires in campaigns that never asked about the property it names.
  Record the outcome in a `pub(crate)` field (group them in one `#[derive(Clone, Default)]` struct)
  and the owning component's test asserts on it. Panicking in `setup()` is fine — that is the
  harness failing, not a property.

- Anchor path conventions (use the API FACTS below — do NOT guess these):
    * `use {{ crate_id }}::*;` brings the crate's generated items into scope. `{{ crate_id }}` is
      the crate id in the facts, NOT the `#[program] pub mod <name>` module name — the
      module name does NOT change these crate-root paths.
{% if idl %}    * `{{ crate_id }}` is GENERATED from the program's IDL (it is already declared for you at the
      top of the file — do NOT write `declare_fuzz_program!`, and do NOT add the program as a
      dependency; it is not one). Instruction/accounts structs and `ID` are exactly as above, but
      account state types live under `{{ crate_id }}::state::*` and IDL-defined types under
      `{{ crate_id }}::types::*` — e.g. `read_anchor_account::<{{ crate_id }}::state::Foo>(&pda)`.
      Anything the program's Rust source has but its IDL does not (helper fns, consts, private
      types) is NOT available: derive it in the fixture instead.
    * An OPTIONAL account (an `Option<Pubkey>` field) that you want ABSENT is written
      `Some(self.program_id)` — NEVER `None`. Anchor represents an absent optional account as the
      program's own id occupying its slot in the account list; a literal `None` is dropped from the
      list instead, so the program receives fewer accounts than it declares and rejects the
      instruction before running any of its logic. That failure is invisible — the action just
      returns `false` forever and every property behind it holds over code that never executed.
      `Some(<a real account>)` is unchanged; this is only how you say "not supplied".
{% endif %}    * Instruction args struct: `instruction::<PascalName>` — snake_case `foo_bar` → `instruction::FooBar`.
    * Accounts struct: `accounts::<PascalName>` — same PascalCase as the instruction.
    * Program id: the `ID` constant; make a Pubkey via `Pubkey::new_from_array(ID.to_bytes())`.
- Read the program source (via the tools) to confirm exact field names, PDA seeds
  (binary vs string), and signer requirements — the API facts + model below are a summary.
- Output ONLY the fixture module source (imports + struct + #[fuzz_fixture] impl).
  Do NOT write `fn main`, `#[invariant_test]`, or `#[crucible_fuzz]` — those are added later.

